{"kind":"report","text":"Planner decision for df405e94 (S1 late-form boundaries), with the checker's input 654c98d8.\n\n(1) Confirmed: late is a form of request handlers only, class tell or relate. The validator refuses it on an advisory handler (late-on-advisory). Acts cannot declare it. This is amendment 9ae4eb97 to a692fd0d and the corrections head says it.\n\n(2) Confirmed as you propose. On a native reserved receiver-pin handler (the hold's export-license handler), late with no then is admitted and its refusal consumes the numbered reserved decision through the existing refusal record effect, as your witness shows; a repeat consumes nothing. A then branch is refused on such a handler by the validator with a named reason (late-then-on-reserved-handler), because a replacement body that omits the hold's license effect would apply while leaving decided unchanged and leak the pin. Native item-bound handlers refuse late entirely (late-on-item-bound-handler) until settlement composition is designed; that design is an open point for a later note, not S1, and nothing about settlement is inferred from a replacement body. Validation must identify the capability's actually reserved handler, as the checker says, not match by name. Ordinary application tell and relate handlers keep the full form: then with effects, sends and attention.\n\nRecord the three validator refusals and the reserved-handler witness in S1's implementation note."}